WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.520
So we unfortunately have to have today what
is a very real but admittedly depressing conversation,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.400
and it's really about the future of the United
States, be it Biden or Trump, who wins in

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.990
November.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.840
And I want to approach this from a slightly
different angle.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.680
So let me let me sort of set it up for you.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.760
The starting point is that in all likelihood,
more than 60 million Americans are going to

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.970
vote for four more years of Donald Trump in
November.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.529
They're going to see they will have seen what
we saw for the last three and a half years

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:36.580
and they're going to say, I'm going to vote
for four more years of that.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:44.670
And the important context here is that certainly,
depending on who wins the election, we are

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.220
changing the direction of the country in the
sense that if Donald Trump gets another four

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.680
years, that's one direction.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.649
If Joe Biden replaces Donald Trump, that's
a completely different direction.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:58.020
But I was having a conversation yesterday
with a former producer, Lewis, and we were

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.989
talking about sort of the outlook for the
country.

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:06.210
And inevitably, these discussions come up
about, you know, if Biden wins, then this

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:11.860
or if the Senate goes to Democrats than that
or if Trump, then this other path goes forward.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.040
And, of course, the November election results
are going to have major consequences.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.390
We've been looking at policy differences over
the last several months.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.299
And when you look at tax policy, yesterday,
Donald Trump during his press briefing saying

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:29.640
that he would actually be cutting the capital
gains tax, whereas Joe Biden wants to tax

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.890
capital gains as ordinary income, which would
be.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.600
I mean, these are really large differences
in policy.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.950
Look at green New Deal elements that Joe Biden
is including in his climate plan versus what

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.079
Donald Trump has done.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:47.770
I mean, just look at almost any policy and
you see that these are very different trajectories.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:55.770
But there is a really big difference in terms
of outcome versus the makeup of the country.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:03.399
And when it comes to voting, in all likelihood,
somewhere between 60 million and 66 million

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:06.970
people are going to vote for Donald Trump.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.129
And maybe the range is a little bigger.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:14.080
Maybe it's like at least 58 million and probably
no more than 68 million people are going to

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.650
vote for Donald Trump, but around 60 million
people are going to say this is the guy I

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:19.650
want.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.870
So the point I'm making is that even if we
set the country on a different path in January

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:31.090
by electing Joe Biden in November, if Donald
Trump is indeed removed, somewhere around

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.810
half of the voters will have voted for Donald
Trump.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.610
And this is not trivial.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:43.040
This has real consequences day to day for
you and for me beyond what the president does.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.380
So certainly, if you think about climate,
putting in Biden instead of Trump will make

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.400
a massive difference.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:56.480
But spending on where you live, half of the
school committee you might be involved in

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:04.200
will be made up of Trump voters, up police
officers, doctors, accountants, the people

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.400
that we are going to be interacting with on
a daily basis.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.739
Even if Joe Biden ekes out a win over Donald
Trump from the standpoint of the Electoral

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:14.080
College.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.319
These folks aren't going away and their ideology
doesn't get changed or fixed because their

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:22.610
candidate loses the Electoral College.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.110
And I don't know what the answer to that is.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:30.940
And we've talked before in these segments
about Trump ism doesn't end when Donald Trump

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.440
leaves office, be it in January or January
of 2025.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.080
That's not just a catchphrase.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.129
It's not trivial.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:39.500
It's not unimportant.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.120
It's extraordinary, really.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.890
And how do we start thinking about that?

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.360
Because it's such a bigger issue.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.870
And, you know, there's this phrase that's
often mentioned at something like Trump didn't

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:55.260
create racists, but they're just more comfortable
out in public right now, much along the same

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:56.260
lines.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.020
Donald Trump did not create anti intellectual
ism or pseudo intellectual ism.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.180
We've done interviews and I've explained to
you that those ideologies have been around

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:09.330
since the eighteen hundreds in some form approximating
what we have today.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.599
And they have been coalesced into a sort of
voting bloc at this point.

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:19.190
We saw it with George W. Bush and we see it
even more with with Donald Trump.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.389
And so this is a bigger picture issue.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:25.080
There's no doubt that much more than George
W. Bush.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.000
Donald Trump has sort of coalesced and emboldened
this non-thinking crowd.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.389
But once the election's over and hopefully
Trump leaves this January, it might not be

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:38.340
until January of 2025.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:47.460
It remains to be seen what happens with that
anti intellectual non-thinking Trump ist ideology

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.229
that doesn't go away.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:50.830
What happens in the Senate?

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.400
What happens in the House of Representatives?

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.170
This is not trivial and it's something that
we really have to think about.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.630
And what no matter who wins the presidency,
there will.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:06.160
Somewhere around 60 to 65 million people who
say what I saw the last three and a half years.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.190
I voted for more of it.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:12.300
Hopefully they won't get it, but we'll still
have to deal with those people.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.169
And that gets me to the next topic I want
to discuss with you today, which actually

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.090
relates to the popular vote sort of in this
context.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:50.110
So let's talk about

00:05:50.110 --> 00:06:33.659
that next.

